# Response to Reviewer 1

We thank the reviewer for the detailed assessment and for recognizing the engineering strength and extensive validation of the work. We address each point below; manuscript locations are given as (Sec/Table X).

## R1-1 — Novelty of the methodological contributions
We agree that "SFT cold-start + RL", GRPO, and interface randomization each have clear precedents, and we have removed any wording that implies these components are individually novel. We now explicitly cite CogCoM and TACO for trajectory/CoM-style data, note that Tool-GRPO applies GRPO without a new RL operator, and cite prior domain-adaptation / meta-learning work for randomization/paraphrasing. We reposition our contribution accordingly: not a new learning algorithm, but (a) the **integration** of these ingredients into a multi-turn, multi-tool orchestration framework, and (b) the **new material of this journal version** — interface-robustness Adaptive Learning (Sec 2.4), the systematic randomized generalization study (Table 4), and the V\*/HRBench tool-planning evaluation (Tables 5-6). See revised Sec 1 and Related Work.

## R1-2 — "Generalize to New Tools" cannot disentangle abstract-function understanding from robust interface mapping
We agree with this critique and have **scoped the claim** rather than overstate it. We no longer claim the model understands "abstract tool functions." We now claim only **robustness to interface-level variation** (renamed / re-described / re-ordered tools while functionality is preserved) together with cross-stage transfer, and we say so explicitly in Sec 3.3 and the Table caption. We argue this is still a deployment-relevant property: in practice tool names, argument schemas, and descriptions change across versions and providers, and brittle interface-coupling is a real failure mode. As supporting evidence we retain the tool-selection analysis (the model adopts useful tools, ignores irrelevant ones, and down-weights redundant ones), which speaks to sensible tool selection under interface change, not to mastery of genuinely novel capabilities.

## R1-3 — Transparency of closed-source model evaluation
We have clarified the protocol (Sec 4.1 and Appendix) and added a table specifying, **for every baseline, whether it received the same tool set, the same system prompt, and the same multi-step protocol**. 〖待补: 依据 W5WP(上一轮)协议填对照表 —— 闭源模型实际是否给了工具/同prompt/几轮作答〗. Where a proprietary model was evaluated without tools, we now label the comparison explicitly (tool-augmented open model vs. no-tool proprietary model) and soften the "surpassing GPT-5" phrasing to the precise, scoped statement it supports.

## R1-4 — Fairness of the DeepEyes / Pixel-Reasoner comparison
We agree these baselines were designed for single-tool / fixed-loop settings. 〖待补 E7: 二选一并填数〗 (a) We re-evaluated them after a **minimal adaptation** that aligns the prompt and tool interface, obtaining [..]; or (b) we explicitly note they were run without adaptation and that the low CPS/success in Table 6 partly reflects prompt/interface incompatibility rather than inherent inferiority. Either way we revise the text so the comparison is not read as a claim of inherent superiority.

## R1-5 — Reward design: theoretical justification and possible reward hacking
We thank the reviewer and want to make explicit a **design intention** that the manuscript stated unclearly. The asymmetric reward is **deliberate**: tool calls incur latency and compute cost, so the reward is designed to elicit tool use **only when it improves correctness** — i.e., cost-aware, need-based tool use. This is precisely the adaptive behaviour the paper studies; it is a **design goal, not reward hacking**. We now state this in Sec 2.3 and support it with evidence (new Table X): (i) on instances solved without tools, accuracy is **not lower** than in the tool-using regime 〖待补 E4: 数字〗; and (ii) on perception/planning-hard instances, the tool-use rate is high 〖待补 E4: 数字〗 — i.e., tools are invoked when they help, not avoided when needed. On theory: we optimize the standard GRPO objective (its convergence properties are inherited); our composite reward is **bounded reward shaping** that biases *when* tools are used and does not change the task optimum. We add this clarification and note the absence of a formal convergence proof as a scoped, honest limitation.

## R1-6 — Multi-turn cost, sample complexity, and error propagation
**(a) Latency/cost.** We now report inference cost directly (Sec 4 / Appendix): end-to-end latency distributions (P50/P90/P99), a per-stage breakdown (generation / tool-execution / orchestration), per-tool times (distinguishing local operators from expert-model calls), and an **accuracy-vs-latency curve at a matched tool budget** against baselines. We also clarify that CPS is not a wall-clock proxy given the mix of local and expert-model tools. 〖待补 E3: 数字/图〗
**(b) Sample complexity as the tool set scales.** We add a discussion and the tool-selection results under an enlarged tool set, showing the model concentrates calls on relevant tools rather than scaling calls with tool-set size. 〖待补: 引用工具选择统计〗
**(c) Error propagation under failed calls.** We add a controlled tool-failure study (see R2-4 / new Table X) measuring whether local errors are detected, ignored, recovered from, or propagated. 〖待补 E5〗
